    {
      "id": 17,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        470,
        700
      ],
      "size": [
        470,
        300
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 16,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "Note"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "TST A/B \u2014 does temporal-attention correction help at all?\n\nWHY THIS GRAPH IS DELIBERATELY BORING\n  One generation, 192 frames, no chunking, no masks, no LoRAs beyond the\n  normal stack. TST corrects cross-frame attention, and the first question is\n  whether it helps a PLAIN render. Testing it inside a chunked or masked graph\n  first would mix that question with a second one -- see the open question\n  below -- and neither answer would be readable.\n\nTHE PROTOCOL, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT\n  1. Queue as shipped. The TST node is BYPASSED, so this is the control.\n  2. Un-bypass H3 Temporal State Transport (ctrl-B). Change NOTHING else --\n     the seed is already `fixed` at 2024, which is what makes the pair\n     comparable.\n  3. Queue again. Watch the console for `[H3 TST]` lines: they report frames,\n     rows per frame, tension before/after, mean gamma, the share of corrected\n     heads, and TST's own runtime. No lines means it never engaged.\n\n  Judge on what TST claims to fix: details that flicker or morph between\n  frames, identity drift, implausible motion. One seed answers \"did anything\n  change\"; it does not answer \"is it better\" -- that needs several seeds.\n\nSETTINGS\n  tau 0.2 is the paper's recommended value. The author says 0.5 is visibly too\n  strong. 0 keeps the diagnostics and disables the correction, which is a\n  useful third arm if the pair looks identical and you suspect it never ran.\n\nWHAT IS VERIFIED, AND WHAT IS NOT\n  Verified by reading this ComfyUI build: the attention override hook exists\n  (attention.py:187), H3's attention passes transformer_options so the hook is\n  reached (minimax/model.py:199), TST rescales q and still calls the real\n  backend so comfy-kitchen keeps working, and it locates video rows from the\n  END of the packed sequence using the latent's own shape -- so it does not\n  read PackedLayout and our interior-keyframe patch cannot confuse it.\n\n  NOT verified: whether it improves anything. That is this render.\n\nTHE OPEN QUESTION, FOR LATER\n  TST measures tension across ALL frames. In a bracket / insert / pin render a\n  large share of those frames are HELD -- clean, finished content sitting next\n  to noisy generated rows -- and whether that mixture skews the measurement is\n  untested. Held rows themselves are safe on 0.35 (model output is multiplied\n  by the denoise mask, so they take no update), but what the GENERATED rows\n  attend to could shift. Answer this graph first, then that one.\n\n  Keep it away from the long-form recipe until both pass: that recipe works,\n  and this modifies attention on every layer."
      ],
      "title": "How to use"
    },
